# crswd configuration — NAMES AND DESCRIPTIONS ONLY.
#
# Every assignment below is deliberately empty. This file is committed and this
# repository is public, so a value written here is a value published forever.
# Copy it to .env (gitignored) for local development, or fill in a copy kept
# outside the repo and point the systemd unit's EnvironmentFile at that — see
# deploy/README.md.
#
# The daemon reads its whole configuration once, before it binds or spawns
# anything, and nothing here can be changed while it runs. Every value that
# would weaken a bound is a startup failure rather than a warning
# (docs/security.md §4), because sessions run with
# --dangerously-skip-permissions and these variables are what stands in for the
# permission prompt that is gone.
#
# internal/config is the source of truth. The names below are pinned to it by
# TestEnvExampleNamesEveryVariable, so a variable added there fails the build
# until it is documented here.
#
# Every setting here can also be written in a configuration file —
# ~/.config/crswd/config, spelled `allowed_roots = ...`: each name below minus
# its CRSW_ prefix, lower-cased. See config.example at the repository root for
# what each one is for and what a wrong value does. The environment wins where
# both set a value, so a unit can override one setting without a file being
# rewritten.

# --- Required. The daemon refuses to start without it. ---

# HMAC shared secret for the API. Minimum 32 bytes; unset or shorter is a
# startup failure. There is deliberately no default — an absent secret is an
# absent authentication layer, and a daemon that starts with auth disabled is
# worse than one that does not start.
#
# Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32` and keep it in 1Password. It is never
# logged, never returned in an error, and never echoed back — not even its
# length.
CRSW_SHARED_SECRET=

# Declare that Cloudflare Access is the browser door: `true` or `false`, and
# anything else is a startup failure. Default: false.
#
# It selects nothing on its own — the three values below have selected the
# Access door since long before this variable existed, and still do. What it
# adds is you saying so, which is checkable where an unstated intention is not:
# set with none of the three, the daemon refuses to start instead of serving a
# dashboard that admits nobody, and set beside CRSW_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD it names
# the collision rather than picking a door for you.
CRSW_ACCESS_ENABLED=

# Your Cloudflare Access team domain — normally `<team>.cloudflareaccess.com`.
# Unset or unusable is a startup failure.
#
# One value, because two things must agree: the daemon derives the expected
# issuer (`https://<host>`) and the key-set URL
# (`https://<host>/cdn-cgi/access/certs`) from it. Configuring them separately
# would allow an issuer and a key set that do not belong to each other, which is
# a validator checking signatures against the wrong authority.
#
# A full origin is accepted too, and `http://` is refused unless the host is a
# loopback IP literal — that carve-out exists so the quickstart and the contract
# tests can serve a key set they control on 127.0.0.1 with no Cloudflare account.
# It skips no validation.
CRSW_ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=

# The Access application's AUD tag, from the application's settings in the
# Cloudflare dashboard. Empty is a startup failure.
#
# It is compared for equality against the assertion's audience and never parsed,
# so an assertion minted for a different application on the same team — or for a
# different team entirely — is refused.
CRSW_ACCESS_AUD=

# Comma-separated list of the email addresses allowed to reach the dashboard. At
# least one is required; empty, or an entry that is empty or carries a space, is
# a startup failure.
#
# The edge is the gate. This is the daemon asserting the gate is configured as
# believed — layer 1 is not weakened by an Access policy someone widened in the
# dashboard and forgot. A refused address never reaches the audit trail.
CRSW_ACCESS_ALLOWED_EMAILS=

# The other browser door: the password for a daemon on an internal network,
# where there is no Cloudflare edge in front of it and so no assertion for
# layer 1 to validate. At least 16 characters; shorter is a startup failure.
# Unset means there is no password door, which is every daemon today.
#
# It is never a door as well as Access. Configured beside CRSW_ACCESS_ENABLED or
# the three values above, the daemon refuses to start rather than choosing.
#
# Treated as a secret: a file that sets it must be mode 0600, /settings reports
# it as present or absent and never spells it, and it cannot be edited from the
# browser — a password settable from the page it protects is not a door.
#
# On a network with no TLS in front of the daemon this password crosses the wire
# in clear on every sign-in. Put a reverse proxy with a certificate in front of
# it, or accept that anyone who can watch the LAN can read it.
CRSW_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD=

# --- Optional. Each falls back to the default named in its description. ---

# Colon-separated (`:`) list of absolute paths. A session may only run in a
# directory contained by one of these, and this is the real blast-radius
# control — keep it narrow.
#
# Default: $HOME/code, announced by a loud banner on stderr at every start.
# $HOME itself is never the default: SSH keys, cloud credentials and browser
# profiles all live directly under it, which would make the allowlist
# decorative.
#
# Fails closed. Every entry must be absolute and must already exist as a
# directory that resolves; one that does not is a startup failure, as is an
# empty entry. Two spellings that resolve to the same directory collapse to
# one. When this is unset, HOME must be set and absolute or startup fails.
CRSW_ALLOWED_ROOTS=

# Whether the create form's working-directory field also offers the immediate
# subdirectories of each approved root as suggestions. Default: off. Anything
# Go's strconv.ParseBool refuses — `yes`, `on`, `2` — is a startup failure
# rather than a silent off.
#
# Off on purpose: listing a filesystem is a disclosure, however mild, and it is
# one to opt into rather than to find already made. It reads one level under
# CRSW_ALLOWED_ROOTS and nowhere else, never follows a link out of a root, and
# is capped.
#
# It widens nothing. CRSW_ALLOWED_ROOTS above is still the control — a suggested
# path is resolved and checked against it on create exactly as a typed one is,
# and the field stays free text either way.
CRSW_DISCOVER_ROOTS=

# Comma-separated (`,`) list of absolute paths the create form offers as
# working-directory suggestions, on top of the approved roots — which are always
# offered — and any discovered children. Default: empty.
#
# Every entry must be absolute; a relative one is a startup failure, because no
# create could ever accept it. An empty entry — a stray comma — is a startup
# failure too, rather than a blank line in the picker. Nothing here is checked
# against the filesystem: these are paths you typed, and offering one says
# nothing about whether it exists.
#
# A path outside CRSW_ALLOWED_ROOTS may be listed, and it is refused on create
# exactly as a typed one is. That is not a contradiction — the list is a
# convenience and the roots are the control.
CRSW_WORKDIR_SUGGESTIONS=

# Additional environment variable NAMES a session receives, comma-separated.
# Default: empty.
#
# A session does not inherit this daemon's environment. It gets a composed one:
# HOME, PATH, SHELL, USER, LOGNAME, TERM, LANG, LC_*, XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and TMUX_TMPDIR, and
# nothing else. The shared secret, the Access values and every CRSW_ setting stay
# on this side of the boundary, because a session runs
# `claude --dangerously-skip-permissions` and anything in its environment is one
# `env` away from being pane content.
#
# This is the escape hatch for a workflow that needs one more — SSH_AUTH_SOCK for
# a session that pushes over SSH is the usual one. It holds NAMES, not values;
# the value comes from this daemon's own environment, so a name it does not have
# is not an error and not an empty variable, it is simply not passed.
#
# Naming a secret, or anything beginning CRSW_, is a startup failure. A silent
# drop would leave you with an escape hatch you believe is working, and a
# warning about a credential is a credential that ships.
CRSW_SESSION_ENVIRONMENT=

# host:port for the HTTP listener. Default: 127.0.0.1:8765.
#
# The host must be an IP literal. A name — including `localhost`, and including
# the empty host of `:8765` — is refused rather than resolved under every door,
# because /etc/hosts or a resolver could move the bind without this value
# changing. The wildcard is spelled 0.0.0.0, which can be read off this line.
#
# Whether a non-loopback literal is accepted depends on the browser door. With
# one configured — Cloudflare Access, or CRSW_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD — it is, so an
# operator on their own network can reach the daemon from the machine they are
# sitting at. With neither, it is a startup failure and loopback is the only
# address: a daemon whose dashboard admits nobody stays where the tunnel can
# find it and nothing else can. The invariant is "never reachable without
# authentication", not "never reachable".
CRSW_LISTEN=

# How many sessions may exist at once. Default: 5. Below 1 is a startup
# failure. A create past the cap is refused with 429 rather than degrading the
# host.
CRSW_MAX_SESSIONS=

# Whether stopping the daemon also destroys every running session.
#
# Default: off. A restart preserves them, and startup adoption reclaims them with
# their deadlines measured from when the work began — so a session cannot outlive
# its lifetime by being handed between daemons. This is what you want while
# updating a binary: a redeploy should not cost you your fleet.
#
# Set to "true" if a stopped daemon should mean a stopped host: nothing left
# running, nothing to reconnect to. Note that a *crash* leaves sessions running
# either way, which is why adoption exists — this setting governs the clean stop
# only.
CRSW_DESTROY_ON_SHUTDOWN=
# How long a session may live from creation. Default: 24h. A create may ask for
# less, or for more up to CRSW_SESSION_LIFETIME_MAX. It must be a positive
# duration: the word "never" that the ceiling below takes is refused here.
#
# That asymmetry is the design. This is the lifetime every session gets without
# asking, so a daemon that read "never" here would be one whose every session is
# immortal by default. The ceiling opens that door and a create walks through it.
CRSW_SESSION_LIFETIME=

# The ceiling a per-session lifetime override may not exceed. Defaults to
# CRSW_SESSION_LIFETIME, so overrides buy nothing until this is raised
# deliberately. Below the default is a startup failure — every create would be
# refused by a bound set without meaning to.
#
# "never" is the one value here that is not a duration: no ceiling at all, and so
# a daemon on which a create may ask for a session that never expires. `0` is
# unset and a negative duration is refused rather than read as a second spelling
# of the word — both are things a person writes meaning "no time at all".
#
# What it costs, plainly. The absolute deadline is the one bound that is never
# renewed, so with it gone the containment left is CRSW_ALLOWED_ROOTS and
# whichever door admits callers, not the reaper. A session created with both
# switches off is reaped by nothing and holds an unsandboxed shell until somebody
# destroys it. It is also what puts the second switch on the dashboard's create
# form: only a daemon whose ceiling is gone offers "never die at the lifetime
# limit", because a create asking for it anywhere else is certain to be refused.
#
# One edge before relying on it: no session record survives the daemon. A restart
# adopts such a session back from tmux as an ordinary one — carrying the built-in
# 24h lifetime, because tmux knows the session and not what was asked for it —
# and tears it down on the spot if it is already older than that.
CRSW_SESSION_LIFETIME_MAX=

# Per-caller create budget, in creates per minute. Default: 6. Below 1 is a
# startup failure.
#
# The burst is derived from this rate as max(1, rate/2) — 3 at the default —
# and is deliberately not a second variable, which could be set in
# disagreement with the first. A create spends a token whatever it goes on to
# answer, so getting the request wrong on purpose is not free.
CRSW_CREATE_RATE_PER_MIN=

# Largest request body the daemon will read, in bytes. Default: 65536. Below 1
# is a startup failure. An oversize body is refused before it is parsed, so it
# never reaches a handler and never reaches the audit trail.
CRSW_MAX_BODY_BYTES=

# How many live output streams may be open at once, across every browser tab.
# Default: 10 — twice the default session cap, so every session on a fully
# loaded host is watchable from two tabs before the daemon starts refusing.
# Below 1 is a startup failure. An open past the cap is refused with 429.
CRSW_MAX_STREAMS=

# The largest screen a pane capture may return, in lines. Default: 200. Below 1
# is a startup failure.
#
# A capture past it is refused rather than shortened: half a screen is a wrong
# screen, not a smaller one — the tail is where the prompt, the error and the
# cursor are — so a session whose pane will not fit reads as "not just now"
# rather than as content that is quietly missing its last lines.
#
# The daemon never attaches to the sessions it starts, so they keep tmux's 80x24
# default and nothing real comes close to this. It is a tripwire on the
# assumption rather than a display limit: a capture that reached into the
# scrollback (tmux's own history-limit is 2000 lines) is refused on the first
# screen instead of silently enlarging every one the daemon believes in.
CRSW_PANE_BOUND=

# The command line typed into a new session's shell. Default:
# `claude --dangerously-skip-permissions` — exactly what every session ran
# before this was configurable, so an operator who sets nothing sees no change.
#
# It is typed at a shell rather than being the tmux session's own command, so a
# crash leaves an inspectable shell and a prompt instead of a vanished session.
# That means what you write here is what runs. It is refused at startup if it is
# empty, if it contains a `;` — tmux's parser eats one before the line is ever
# typed, so it would be delivered silently truncated — or if it contains a
# control character, which would submit half a line.
#
# Read `docs/security.md` before changing it. Both this and any entry in
# CRSW_START_COMMANDS run unsupervised in an unsandboxed shell, and the daemon's
# audit trail sees only what the daemon itself did.
CRSW_START_COMMAND=

# The named set a create may choose from, as `name=command line` entries
# separated by commas. Default: empty — only the `default` command above exists.
#
#   CRSW_START_COMMANDS=rc=claude --dangerously-skip-permissions "/rc"
#
# A create carries a NAME (`"start_command":"rc"`), never a command line: a
# create route that took a command line would make this daemon a general
# remote-shell service behind whatever authenticates that route. An unknown name
# is refused with 400 and starts nothing.
#
# `default` is always present. Naming it here is how you set it alongside the
# others — but setting it here *and* in CRSW_START_COMMAND is a startup failure,
# because which of two spellings wins is the last thing to leave to chance. Each
# entry is validated exactly as CRSW_START_COMMAND is; a name is [a-z0-9-] and
# at most 32 characters, because a name reaches the audit trail. A command line
# may not contain a comma.
#
# A command line may carry `{name}`, and nothing else in braces. It is replaced
# with the session's own name, so the session an operator names here is the
# session they find in claude.ai:
#
#   CRSW_START_COMMANDS=rc=claude --dangerously-skip-permissions "/rc {name}"
#
# That `rc` line is an ordinary interactive session that drives itself into
# remote control, so the tmux session *is* the session. The other spelling,
# `claude remote-control --spawn=same-dir --name {name}`, is still supported and
# still starts a remote-controlled session — but `--spawn` puts the conversation
# on the relay and leaves this daemon's pane empty, and an empty pane is a
# dashboard that cannot show, judge, or compact anything. The quotes are part of
# the command line the shell is handed, so check that whatever reads this file
# for you — a shell's `export`, systemd's `EnvironmentFile=` — leaves them on
# it; the `start_commands` line in config.example takes the value verbatim.
#
# That substitution is safe because a session name is `^[a-zA-Z0-9-]{1,64}$` —
# no space, quote, semicolon, backtick or newline — so no name can change the
# shape of the line, only the argument it already had. Any other `{word}` is a
# startup failure naming the token, because a typo like `{worrking_dir}` typed
# at a shell is a brace expansion nobody asked for. `${HOME}` is the shell's own
# and passes through untouched.
CRSW_START_COMMANDS=

# Which of the names above the dashboard's remote-control switch turns on.
# Default: `rc`.
#
# The switch is a mode, not a menu: the create form asks whether remote control
# is on, and this variable is where an operator reads and changes what that
# means. Off maps to `default`, on maps to this name.
#
# Left unset on a daemon that configures no `rc` command, there is no remote
# control to offer and the form renders no switch. Set to a name
# CRSW_START_COMMANDS does not define, the daemon refuses to start — a switch
# that quietly started plain sessions is worse than one that is absent.
CRSW_REMOTE_CONTROL_COMMAND=

# --- Not configurable, and named here so nobody goes looking for a knob. ---
#
# The absolute session lifetime was a constant here and is not any more: it is
# CRSW_SESSION_LIFETIME above, with a _MAX ceiling beside it that bounds what one
# create may ask for. There was an idle timeout too, with the same pair of
# variables, until milestone 15 withdrew the bound; a file still setting either
# is refused at startup and `crswd config migrate` removes them. The
# session token's lifetime is still the session's absolute deadline by
# construction, so it follows that setting rather than being a second one — and
# on a session whose absolute deadline was removed, the token does not expire
# either. What keeps the host bounded is no longer that the durations are
# unwritable — it is that the ceiling is the operator's and the choice under it
# is the session's, and that the one value spelling "never" is written on the
# ceiling, where an operator removes a bound deliberately, and nowhere a create
# can reach on its own.
#
# These four are constants in the code, and each is a bound rather than a
# preference — a deployment that could widen one from an environment file could
# unbound the host:
#
#   - the signed-request timestamp window, 300s in both directions
#   - the sign-in budget, 6 attempts a minute per source address
#   - the dashboard session cookie's lifetime, 12h
#   - the minimum dashboard password, 16 characters
#
# The last three are the password door's, and a variable that could set the
# budget to 6000 or the minimum to 1 would be a knob on the one route standing
# in front of layer 1.
#
# There is no variable that disables Access validation. The development bypass
# is a build tag and a flag (`go build -tags dev`, `--dev-auth-bypass`), absent
# from the shipping binary rather than defaulted off, because a production
# artifact that can disable authentication from its environment is a backdoor.
